#include "curriculum.h"
/* Original course-specific additions: complementary to tutorialChapters.
 * Examples are for study; C++ samples are not compiled by the C11 app. */
const TutorialChapter extraChapters[24] = {
    { {
        {"Compile, run, and troubleshoot", "A C source file is not run directly. GCC first checks syntax and types, then links the program with libraries. Compiler errors usually indicate invalid code; linker errors mean a referenced function or library could not be resolved. The -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic flags help catch mistakes early.", "gcc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic hello.c -o hello\n./hello", "If hello.c has a valid main and prints a greeting, the terminal displays that greeting. On Windows the executable is hello.exe."},
        {"Output and newline escapes", "printf uses a format string. A backslash followed by n means a newline, whereas a literal slash character is not a newline. Always provide values corresponding to conversion specifiers, and use %% to print a percent sign.", "#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"Score: %d%%\\n\", 85);\n    printf(\"Ready\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}", "Output is Score: 85% on one line and Ready on the next. Exercise: add your own label and value."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Type conversions and integer arithmetic", "When both operands are integers, division truncates towards zero. A floating-point result requires a floating-point operand, often introduced with an explicit cast. Use sizeof to inspect an implementation's type sizes instead of assuming all systems use the same sizes.", "#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n    int earned=7, total=2;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", earned/total);\n    printf(\"%.1f\\n\", (double)earned/total);\n}", "The outputs are 3 and 3.5. Casting after integer division is too late: (double)(earned/total) would print 3.0."},
        {"Read input safely", "scanf needs an address for numeric objects so it can write the parsed value. Check the number of items converted; otherwise invalid input can leave the variable unchanged. For arrays of char, use a width limit when reading words to avoid overflow.", "int age = 0;\nprintf(\"Enter age: \" );\nif (scanf(\"%d\", &age) == 1) {\n    printf(\"Age: %d\\n\", age);\n} else {\n    puts(\"Please enter an integer\");\n}", "The success branch runs only when an integer is read. This follows the input examples in your introductory C notes."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Conditional logic in exam-style tasks", "Translate a specification into an ordered set of conditions. A leap year is divisible by 400, or divisible by 4 but not 100. The parentheses make the intended precedence visible. Compare with == instead of accidentally assigning with =.", "int isLeapYear(int y) {\n    return (y % 400 == 0) ||\n           ((y % 4 == 0) && (y % 100 != 0));\n}", "2000 is a leap year, 1900 is not, and 2024 is. These cases come directly from the Lab 1 requirements."},
        {"Nested loops and pattern tracing", "For a triangle, the outer loop selects the row and the inner loop controls how many symbols to print. For prime testing, check candidate divisors while divisor <= number/divisor to avoid squaring overflow for large positive integers.", "for (int row=1; row<=4; ++row) {\n    for (int col=0; col<row; ++col)\n        putchar('A');\n    putchar('\\n');\n}", "Output: A, AA, AAA, AAAA on separate lines. The nested-loop version matches the triangle exercise in Lab 1."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Plan test cases before coding", "A useful function contract names its input, return value and corner cases. For the prime-check lab, reject numbers below 2 and test divisibility only while candidates could still reveal a factor. Small tests uncover logical errors before more complicated cases.", "int prime(int n) {\n    if (n < 2) return 0;\n    for (int d=2; d<=n/d; ++d)\n        if (n%d==0) return 0;\n    return 1;\n}", "prime(2) is 1, prime(9) is 0 and prime(1) is 0. This adapts isAPrimeNumber from the uploaded Lab 1."},
        {"Passing values versus modifying objects", "Calling a function with an int copies the value. To modify a caller-owned int in C, pass its address and dereference a valid pointer inside the function. In C++ you may also pass an int& reference. The distinction matters for lab functions that insert or delete elements.", "void increment(int *value) {\n    if (value != NULL) ++*value;\n}\nint count=4;\nincrement(&count);", "count becomes 5. Compare this with a function declared void increment(int value), which changes only a local copy."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Search, rotate and reverse arrays", "A linear search visits entries one by one, returning -1 when the target is absent. Reversing an array exchanges symmetric elements until the midpoint. Rotation must preserve the first element before shifting other elements.", "int find(const int *a, int n, int value) {\n    for (int i=0; i<n; ++i)\n        if (a[i]==value) return i;\n    return -1;\n}", "find({3,9,2},3,9) conceptually returns index 1 (pass a real int array). These tasks correspond to find, reverse and rotateLeft in Lab 1."},
        {"Strings, capacity and terminators", "In a C string, the final zero byte is part of the stored representation but not part of strlen's count. A five-character word needs at least six char elements. Prefer snprintf for formatted string creation because it accepts a destination capacity.", "char name[8] = \"Ada\";\nprintf(\"%zu\\n\", strlen(name));\nprintf(\"%c\\n\", name[3] == '\\0' ? 'Y' : 'N');", "The outputs are 3 and Y (include <stdio.h> and <string.h>). The fourth element is the terminator, not a visible character."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Matrix indexing and bounds", "C stores a multi-dimensional array in row-major order: each complete row comes before the next. A 4 by 6 matrix has valid row indices 0..3 and column indices 0..5. Passing it to a function requires its inner dimension so pointer arithmetic can find the next row.", "int sum4x6(const int grid[4][6]) {\n    int sum=0;\n    for (int r=0; r<4; ++r)\n        for (int c=0; c<6; ++c)\n            sum += grid[r][c];\n    return sum;\n}", "This visits exactly 24 cells without exceeding the bounds. It prepares for the 4x6 grid tasks in Lab 1."},
        {"Detect clusters before replacing them", "When searching a grid for three matching entries, check the horizontal run first, then the vertical run as requested. Ignore excluded sentinel values; test that the row or column leaves enough space before reading the next two elements.", "if (c+2 < 6 && grid[r][c] > 0 &&\n    grid[r][c] == grid[r][c+1] &&\n    grid[r][c] == grid[r][c+2]) {\n    /* handle a horizontal match */\n}", "The bounds test prevents out-of-bounds access. Next implement your worksheet's ReplaceWithMinusOne and FallDownAndReplace stages."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Draw a pointer memory diagram", "Think of memory as labelled locations. The pointer variable contains an address, and dereferencing follows that address to the object. The pointer and the pointed-to object occupy distinct storage; changing the pointed-to value does not automatically change the pointer.", "int score=37;\nint *p=&score;\n*p=40;\nprintf(\"%d %d\\n\", score, *p);", "The two values are both 40. Compare with the diagrams in Pointers.pdf pages 3-8 showing addresses and contents."},
        {"Null, invalid and dangling pointers", "A null pointer deliberately points to no object; test it before dereferencing. An uninitialized pointer has an indeterminate value. A dangling pointer once referred to an object that no longer exists. These are different cases but none may be safely dereferenced.", "int *p=NULL;\nif (p != NULL) {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", *p);\n} else {\n    puts(\"No value available\");\n}", "The program prints No value available. Do not attempt to inspect a freed or out-of-scope object through its former pointer."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Trace pointer swaps", "Your lab requires swap_ints and swap_chars. The function receives addresses; *a and *b access the original values. A temporary variable must store the first value before it is overwritten.", "void swap_chars(char *a, char *b) {\n    char temp=*a;\n    *a=*b;\n    *b=temp;\n}\nchar left='A', right='B';\nswap_chars(&left, &right);", "Afterward left is B and right is A. Try tracing values at each assignment before running it."},
        {"Pointer-to-pointer when head changes", "A pointer argument allows a function to change a caller-owned value. When the value to change is itself a pointer, use a pointer-to-pointer in C. This is why linked-list insertion functions often take Node **head.", "void clear_head(int **head) {\n    *head=NULL;\n}\nint value=5;\nint *p=&value;\nclear_head(&p);", "p becomes NULL; value still exists and equals 5. Only the pointer was changed. Avoid discarding dynamically allocated memory without freeing it."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Walk arrays with two pointers", "The pointer lab forbids square-bracket indexing inside the required functions. Set one pointer to the first entry and another to the last entry, then swap until they meet. Do not form arr+size-1 for an empty array without checking size first.", "void reverse_array(int *arr, int size) {\n    if (size <= 1) return;\n    int *left=arr, *right=arr+size-1;\n    while (left < right) {\n        int tmp=*left; *left=*right; *right=tmp;\n        ++left; --right;\n    }\n}", "For {1,2,3,4,5}, the array becomes {5,4,3,2,1}. This is the reverse_array task from the pointers lab."},
        {"Find a maximum without subscripts", "Start from the first value, then compare each remaining value using a moving pointer. Decide what your function should do for size <= 0: an int return value cannot represent failure and any possible maximum unambiguously.", "int find_max(const int *arr, int size) {\n    if (size <= 0) return 0; /* chosen contract */\n    int maximum=*arr;\n    for (const int *p=arr+1; p<arr+size; ++p)\n        if (*p>maximum) maximum=*p;\n    return maximum;\n}", "For {3,9,2,8,5} the result is 9. Clearly document that this example uses 0 as its empty-input fallback."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Copy and append using char pointers", "When copying, including the terminating zero is essential. One short idiom copies the current character, including the eventual zero, then stops. Concatenation first finds the terminator in the destination and then copies the source there.", "void copy_string(char *dest, const char *src) {\n    while ((*dest++ = *src++) != '\\0') {}\n}", "With sufficient destination capacity, copying Hello results in the six bytes H e l l o and terminating zero. This follows copy_string in the pointer lab."},
        {"String comparison and vowels", "Lexicographic comparison stops at the first unequal character or the terminator. Cast char values to unsigned char before comparing character codes to avoid negative-char surprises. Counting vowels can be case-insensitive after normalizing a character with tolower.", "int compare_strings(const char *a,const char *b) {\n    while (*a && *a==*b) { ++a; ++b; }\n    return (unsigned char)*a-(unsigned char)*b;\n}", "compare_strings(\"apple\",\"banana\") is negative. The pointer lab also asks you to count vowels and find string lengths without indexing."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Understand variable lifetime", "A local automatic variable stops existing when execution leaves its block. Even if a debugger still shows the old bytes, dereferencing a pointer to that expired object is undefined behavior. Safe alternatives include returning a value or writing into caller-provided storage.", "int getFive(void) {\n    int value=5;\n    return value; /* safe: return a copy */\n}", "The caller receives 5 without a dangling pointer. Compare this with the invalid return &local example in your memory-management lecture."},
        {"Ownership, leaks and use-after-free", "A memory leak happens when an allocation becomes unreachable without being released. Use-after-free happens when code accesses released storage. They are distinct bugs. In C++, use RAII types like vector or unique_ptr where ownership is required; in C, establish clear free responsibilities.", "int *p=malloc(sizeof *p);\nif (p != NULL) {\n    *p=42;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", *p);\n    free(p);\n    p=NULL;\n}", "The integer is printed before its storage is freed. Include <stdlib.h> and <stdio.h>. Setting p to NULL does not reset any other aliases."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Allocate exactly enough memory", "malloc returns a void pointer suitable for assignment to an object pointer in C. Multiply the number of elements by sizeof *ptr so the allocation follows the actual pointed-to type. Check NULL before writing. free(NULL) is allowed.", "size_t n=5;\nint *a=malloc(n * sizeof *a);\nif (!a) return 1;\nfor (size_t i=0;i<n;++i) a[i]=(int)i;\nfree(a);", "The buffer can store exactly five int values. Include <stdlib.h>. For very large n, guard against size_t multiplication overflow."},
        {"C++ ownership choices", "new/new[] and delete/delete[] must be correctly paired. If object lifetime can be represented with standard containers, prefer that over manual new/delete. A C++ vector changes size safely and frees its owned memory on destruction.", "#include <vector>\nstd::vector<int> values(5, 0);\nvalues[0]=42;\n// no delete[] necessary", "The vector manages its own allocation. This is C++ example code for study; the CodeQuest application itself remains C11."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Draw and traverse a node chain", "A singly linked list has a head pointer and nodes that each contain a value and a next pointer. Following next performs traversal in O(n) time. Unlike arrays, you cannot jump directly to the k-th node in O(1) time.", "Node *current=head;\nwhile (current != NULL) {\n    printf(\"%d \", current->data);\n    current=current->next;\n}", "A chain 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> NULL prints 10 20 30. This matches the sequence illustrated in the linked-list lecture."},
        {"Why lists must own their nodes", "If you allocate each node with malloc in C, each must eventually be freed. If you allocate with new in C++, use delete for each node. Never free a node created as an automatic local variable or share an owning raw pointer without a clear policy.", "Node *node=malloc(sizeof *node);\nif (node) {\n    node->data=10; node->next=NULL;\n    /* attach it or free it */\n}", "The allocation has one node of storage. The owner must later remove the node and call free exactly once."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Prepend to an empty list", "When head is NULL, the same prepend algorithm works: the new node's next becomes NULL, and the head becomes the new node. Passing &head lets the function update the caller's variable in C.", "Node *head=NULL;\nNode first={10,NULL};\npush_front(&head,&first);\n/* head points at first */", "The list contains just 10. The example uses an automatic node, so do not free it."},
        {"Insertion order and head updates", "The most frequent mistake is overwriting head before saving the old first node. Always make newNode->next point to the old head before assigning head=newNode. This means repeated prepends reverse the order of input.", "Node *head=NULL;\nNode a={1,NULL}, b={2,NULL};\npush_front(&head,&a);\npush_front(&head,&b);", "Traversal visits 2 then 1. Sketch head and both next links to prove no node was lost."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Append with correct edge cases", "Append handles two cases: empty list, where head changes, and nonempty list, where you walk to the last node. Forgetting to initialize newNode->next can make traversal follow an invalid address.", "Node *head=NULL;\nNode a={5,NULL};\nappend(&head,&a);\nNode b={9,NULL};\nappend(&head,&b);", "The list becomes 5 -> 9 -> NULL. Its last next pointer is always NULL."},
        {"Insert at a chosen position", "For zero-based insertion, position 0 is a prepend. Otherwise traverse to position-1, link the new node to the successor and then update the predecessor's next. Reject positions beyond the list length unless your specification says otherwise.", "/* insert n immediately after prev */\nn->next=prev->next;\nprev->next=n;", "Between 10 and 30, inserting 20 produces 10 -> 20 -> 30. The lab extends this concept to InsertAtPosition."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Delete the head without losing the list", "Save the next link before freeing the old head. If the list is empty, do nothing. Ensure the node was dynamically allocated with the matching allocator before calling free or delete.", "if (head != NULL) {\n    Node *old=head;\n    head=head->next;\n    free(old); /* only malloc-owned nodes */\n}", "After removing the head from 10 -> 20 -> NULL, head points at 20. Never access old->next after free(old)."},
        {"Remove a middle node", "Keep both prev and current pointers while searching. When a match is found, point prev->next to current->next before freeing current. If there is no previous node, update head instead.", "Node *prev=NULL, *cur=head;\nwhile (cur && cur->data!=target) {\n    prev=cur; cur=cur->next;\n}\nif (cur) {\n    if(prev) prev->next=cur->next;\n    else head=cur->next;\n    free(cur);\n}", "Test empty, first, middle, last and missing-value cases. This snippet assumes malloc-owned nodes."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Maintain both directions", "Every insertion must keep next and prev links consistent. When A->next is B, B->prev must be A. Head's prev and tail's next should be null. Missing one update can make reverse traversal fail.", "DNode a={1,NULL,NULL};\nDNode b={2,&a,NULL};\na.next=&b;", "The two-node list can be traversed in either direction. The fields here are value, prev and next in that order."},
        {"Insert at a position in a doubly linked list", "To insert a new node between left and right, initialize the new node's two pointers, then repair both neighbors. Handle null neighbors at the beginning and end; keep head and tail pointers synchronized.", "n->prev=left; n->next=right;\nif (left) left->next=n; else head=n;\nif (right) right->prev=n; else tail=n;", "This general insertion pattern underlies the DoublyLinkedList class required by the supplied pointer/linked-list lab."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Constructors and access control", "A class constructor establishes valid state. public members are callable from outside the class; private members are available only to the class and its friends. Good encapsulation means callers request operations rather than directly modifying internals.", "class BankAccount {\n    int balance;\npublic:\n    explicit BankAccount(int b):balance(b) {}\n    int getBalance() const { return balance; }\n};", "getBalance reads state without changing it. The snippet is C++ study material, compiled with g++, not with the app's GCC C11 task."},
        {"Copying objects that own resources", "A class with a raw owning pointer must define correct copying, moving and destruction or avoid copying. This is one reason standard C++ containers are safer for teaching dynamic data than handwritten memory-owning classes.", "#include <vector>\nstruct Scores {\n    std::vector<int> values;\n};\nScores a{{1,2}}, b=a;", "b contains its own vector with 1 and 2, not a second owner of the same raw allocation."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Deduction and template requirements", "A function template substitutes concrete types when called. With the common T maximum(T,T) form, both arguments must deduce the same T unless an explicit conversion or template argument is provided. The substituted operations must be valid for that type.", "template <typename T>\nT square(T value) { return value*value; }\nint x=square(4);\ndouble y=square(1.5);", "x is 16 and y is 2.25. This is the same reusable-algorithm principle explained in the templates lecture."},
        {"Generic class state", "A class template can store a member of type T and provide methods using that type. For numeric examples, value-initialize the state as T{} so the accumulator starts at zero for arithmetic types.", "template <typename T>\nclass Adder {\n    T total{};\npublic:\n    void add(T n) { total+=n; }\n    T sum() const { return total; }\n};", "Adder<int> and Adder<double> are distinct specializations. The code requires C++, while the CodeQuest interface stays in C."},
    } },
    { {
        {"What can be overloaded", "An overloaded operator must involve a class or enumeration type and cannot change an operator's precedence or arity. Operator overloads should preserve intuitive meaning. Your uploaded lecture demonstrates adding two Box objects and later increment operators.", "struct Vec2 {\n    int x,y;\n    Vec2 operator+(const Vec2& rhs) const {\n        return {x+rhs.x,y+rhs.y};\n    }\n};", "Vec2{1,2}+Vec2{3,4} produces {4,6}. This member overload is C++ and cannot be compiled as plain C."},
        {"Prefix and postfix are distinct", "The prefix increment signature takes no dummy argument; postfix receives an unused int marker to select its overload. The usual convention is that prefix returns the updated object and postfix returns the old value.", "Counter& operator++() {\n    ++value; return *this;\n}\nCounter operator++(int) {\n    Counter old=*this;\n    ++value; return old;\n}", "These implementations belong inside a class that defines a value member. Compare them with the Clock increment examples in the lecture."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Vector growth and iterator safety", "A vector's size counts actual elements; capacity counts reserved storage. push_back can trigger reallocation. If reallocation occurs, pointers, references and iterators into the old storage become invalid, so obtain them again after growth.", "std::vector<int> v{1,2};\nv.reserve(10);\nv.push_back(3);\nstd::cout << v.size();", "size is 3 and capacity is at least 10. This is C++ study code requiring <vector> and <iostream>."},
        {"Use algorithms with iterators", "An iterator acts like a position in a container. Use begin and end to describe a half-open range [begin,end). std::sort needs random-access iterators, which vector provides. Check that you do not dereference end.", "std::vector<int> v{9,1,5};\nstd::sort(v.begin(),v.end());\nfor (int x:v) std::cout << x << ' ';", "The output is 1 5 9 (include <vector>, <algorithm>, <iostream>). This connects the STL algorithms and iterators lecture sections."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Sort and splice lists", "std::list models a linked sequence and supports push_front, push_back, sort and splice. You cannot index into a list with [i], and general std::sort does not work on its bidirectional iterators. List splice transfers nodes without element copies.", "std::list<int> a{3,1,2};\na.sort();\nstd::list<int> b{8,9};\na.splice(a.end(),b);", "a becomes 1,2,3,8,9 and b is empty. Include <list>. Spliced-element iterators remain valid and now refer to elements of a."},
        {"Complexity choices for lists", "Searching for the nth element of a list takes O(n), but inserting at a known position is constant-time for a single element. A vector supports O(1) random indexing but inserting in the middle usually shifts many elements.", "std::list<int> nums{10,20};\nauto it=nums.begin();\n++it;\nnums.insert(it,15);", "nums becomes 10,15,20. These trade-offs help explain when a linked-list representation is useful."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Ordered maps and lookup", "A map stores a unique key with an associated value. A typical map uses ordering and provides logarithmic find/insert complexity. operator[] inserts a default value for a missing key; use find to test without creating one.", "std::map<std::string,int> scores;\nscores[\"Ada\"]=12;\nif (scores.find(\"Bob\") == scores.end()) {\n    /* Bob not present */\n}", "The map still has just Ada. Include <map> and <string>. The STL maps lecture explores similar key-value operations."},
        {"Set, queue and stack", "A set holds unique ordered values. A queue is first-in first-out and a stack is last-in first-out. All use different interfaces, so choose based on required access operations rather than treating them as interchangeable lists.", "std::queue<int> q;\nq.push(10); q.push(20);\nint first=q.front();\nq.pop();", "first is 10, and now 20 is at the front. Include <queue>. For a stack, top() and pop() operate at the latest pushed element."},
    } },
    { {
        {"Compare realistic costs", "Array/vector indexing is constant-time; scanning a sequence is linear. Sorting n elements takes O(n log n) comparisons. A sorted map lookup is typically logarithmic. Picking a container is about the operations you do most frequently, not one universally fastest choice.", "/* Pseudocode comparison */\n/* vector[i]            O(1) */\n/* search unsorted data O(n) */\n/* map.find(key)        O(log n) */", "The complexity estimates help choose between STL containers, loops and explicit linked lists for exam tasks."},
        {"Connect algorithms with tasks", "Your Lab 1 asks for finding elements, finding the second largest value, duplicate counting and two-movie pairing. Begin with a correct simple method, then improve complexity if needed; for example, use a set to track candidates when solving two-sum style tasks.", "int twoMovies(int total,const int *a,int n) {\n    for(int i=0;i<n;++i)\n        for(int j=i+1;j<n;++j)\n            if(a[i]+a[j]==total) return 1;\n    return 0;\n}", "This checks two distinct indices and takes O(n^2) time. Test with [90,110], target 200 and with an impossible target."},
    } },
};
